---
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const install = `pnpm add @schmooky/zvuk`;

const create = `import { createEngine } from '@schmooky/zvuk';

export const engine = createEngine({
  buses: {
    music: { level: 0.8 },
    sfx:   { level: 1.0 },
    ui:    { level: 0.7 },
  },
  master: { headroom: -3 },  // dB
});`;

const unlock = `document.querySelector('button')!.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  await engine.unlock();
  await engine.loadSound('coin', ['/sfx/coin.webm', '/sfx/coin.m4a'], { bus: 'sfx' });
  engine.sound('coin').play();
});`;

const playFade = `const v = engine.sound('coin').play({
  volume: { jitter: 0.05 },
  pitch:  { jitter: 0.04 },
});

// Bus-level fade — click-free (duration in seconds)
engine.bus('music').fadeTo(0.1, 0.8);

// Voice-level fade with curve
await v.fade({ to: 0, duration: 0.8, curve: 'equal-power' });
await v.ended;`;

const close = `await engine.close();`;
---
<Docs
  title="Quickstart"
  description="Two function calls and a user gesture."
  eyebrow="Start here"
>
  <h2>1. Install</h2>
  <Snippet code={install} lang="bash" />

  <h2>2. Construct the engine</h2>
  <p>
    Declare your buses up front. <code>createEngine</code> does not touch the
    AudioContext. It records the shape of the mix and nothing else, so calling
    it at module load is safe.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={create} lang="ts" />

  <h2>3. Unlock from a user gesture</h2>
  <p>
    Browsers block audio until the user does something. Call
    <code>engine.unlock()</code> from a click, tap or keypress handler. It is
    idempotent, so calling it from several handlers is fine.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={unlock} lang="ts" />

  <p>
    Pass an array of URLs to ship a codec ladder. The first one your browser
    can decode wins. <a href="/guides/asset-formats/">Asset formats</a>
    explains why that matters more than it sounds.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Play, fade, mix</h2>
  <p>
    Every time-valued option in zvuk is in <strong>seconds</strong>. Fades,
    crossfades, attack and release, snapshot applies, all of them. That
    matches the Web Audio API, so there is one convention to remember rather
    than two.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={playFade} lang="ts" />

  <h2>5. Clean up</h2>
  <p>
    On an SPA route change or a game teardown, call
    <code>engine.close()</code>. Every voice stops, the AudioContext closes,
    and listeners detach. Close is terminal. Construct a new engine if you
    need audio again.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={close} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/concepts/engine/">The Engine</a> covers the full lifecycle, state machine and scheduler.</li>
    <li><a href="/concepts/bus/">Bus</a> covers routing, concurrency and sidechain.</li>
    <li><a href="/recipes/">Recipes</a> has six patterns you can click and copy.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/asset-formats/">Asset formats</a> walks through webm/Opus and m4a/AAC.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
